theorem sides-equal
  note        Each side of an order is worked out before the two are
              compared: a sum of a product and a power, equal to the other.
  then 3·4 + 2^3 ≤ 20

1.  3·4 + 2^3 ≤ 20
    arithmetic

theorem sides-below
  note        A power below a sum with a product in it.
  then 2^10 < 1000 + 5·5

1.  2^10 < 1000 + 5·5
    arithmetic

theorem fractions-below
  note        Two fractions compared by their cross products.
  then 2/3 < 5/7

1.  2/3 < 5/7
    arithmetic

theorem fractions-equal
  note        Two spellings of one fraction, at most each other.
  then 2/4 ≤ 3/6

1.  2/4 ≤ 3/6
    arithmetic

theorem fraction-at-most-a-whole-number
  note        A whole number on one side is a fraction over 1.
  then (3 + 4)/2 ≤ 2^2

1.  (3 + 4)/2 ≤ 2^2
    arithmetic

theorem product-over-a-range
  note        A product over a range of numerals is worked a factor at a
              time, each factor a difference.
  then ∏(k ∈ {0, …, 3}) (7 − k) ≤ 840

1.  ∏(k ∈ {0, …, 3}) (7 − k) ≤ 840
    arithmetic

theorem twenty-three-factors-as-a-product
  note        The birthday problem's comparison, its 23 factors written as
              one product.
  then 2·∏(k ∈ {0, …, 22}) (365 − k) < 365^23

1.  2·∏(k ∈ {0, …, 22}) (365 − k) < 365^23
    arithmetic

theorem twenty-three-factors-as-a-fraction
  note        The same comparison as the birthday problem's ratio.
  then (∏(k ∈ {0, …, 22}) (365 − k))/365^23 < 1/2

1.  (∏(k ∈ {0, …, 22}) (365 − k))/365^23 < 1/2
    arithmetic
